::GEO_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size while preserving angle measures? {
=A. Dilation#A dilation scales lengths but preserves angle measures, so it supports similarity.
~B. Translation#A translation preserves size and shape.
~C. Reflection#A reflection preserves size and shape.
~D. Rotation#A rotation preserves size and shape.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence says translate left 4 units, then dilate by 3. Which transformation occurs first? {
~A. Dilate by 3#This reverses the stated order.
=B. Translate left 4 units#Transformation sequences are followed in the stated order.
~C. Reflect across the x-axis#No reflection appears in the sequence.
~D. Rotate 180&deg;#No rotation appears in the sequence.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A pattern slides horizontally and matches the next copy exactly. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry uses a mirror line.
~B. Rotation symmetry#Rotation symmetry uses a turn.
=C. Translation symmetry#A repeated slide that maps a pattern onto itself shows translation symmetry.
~D. Dilation symmetry#Dilation changes size and is not the slide described.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle is dilated by scale factor 2 and then reflected. What relationship does the final image have to the original? {
~A. It must be congruent to the original.#The scale factor 2 changes side lengths, so congruence is not guaranteed.
~B. It has no related angles.#Dilations and reflections preserve angle measures.
~C. It cannot be compared.#The transformation sequence gives a direct comparison.
=D. It is similar to the original.#The dilation preserves shape and the reflection is rigid, so similarity is preserved.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A snowflake-like design maps onto itself after a 60&deg; turn. What type of symmetry is this? {
=A. Rotation symmetry#Mapping onto itself after a turn shows rotation symmetry.
~B. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry requires a mirror line.
~C. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry requires a slide.
~D. Scale symmetry#The description involves a turn, not a change in size.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence says rotate 90&deg;, reflect across the x-axis, then translate up 2. Which step is second? {
~A. Rotate 90&deg;#The rotation is the first step.
=B. Reflect across the x-axis#The second listed transformation is the reflection.
~C. Translate up 2#The translation is the third step.
~D. Dilate by 2#No dilation is included in this sequence.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best justifies similarity using transformations? {
~A. A translation changes all angle measures.#Translations preserve angle measures and do not change shape.
~B. A reflection changes side ratios.#Reflections preserve lengths and do not create proportional scaling.
=C. A dilation followed by a rotation maps one figure to the other.#A dilation can change size while preserving shape, and rotation preserves shape.
~D. A rotation changes side lengths.#Rotations preserve side lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A capital letter has a vertical mirror line through its center. Which symmetry can it have? {
~A. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry involves repeated sliding copies.
~B. Dilation symmetry#A dilation changes size rather than creating mirror halves.
~C. Sequence symmetry#Sequence is not the name of this symmetry type.
=D. Reflection symmetry#A mirror line through the center shows reflection symmetry.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have &angle;A &cong; &angle;D and &angle;B &cong; &angle;E. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
=A. AA similarity#Two congruent angle pairs are enough to prove triangle similarity.
~B. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires all three side ratios.
~C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity requires two proportional side pairs and the included angle.
~D. HL congruence#HL proves congruence in right triangles, not similarity here.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Corresponding sides of two triangles are 3 and 6, 5 and 10, and 7 and 14. Which criterion applies? {
~A. AA similarity#No angle information is given.
=B. SSS similarity#All three corresponding side ratios are 2, so SSS similarity applies.
~C. SAS similarity#The evidence gives all three side ratios, not two sides and an included angle.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence, not to establish similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have a congruent included angle and adjacent side ratios both equal 4. Which criterion applies? {
~A. AA similarity#Only one angle pair is described.
~B. SSS similarity#Only two side ratios are described.
=C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity uses two proportional side pairs and the congruent included angle.
~D. SSS congruence#The side lengths are proportional, not necessarily equal.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>If &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;DEF, which angle corresponds to &angle;B? {
~A. &angle;D#A corresponds to D, not B.
~B. &angle;F#C corresponds to F, not B.
~C. &angle;A#This is an angle in the first triangle, not the matching angle in the second.
=D. &angle;E#The order of the similarity statement maps A to D, B to E, and C to F.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have side ratios 2, 2, and 3. Is SSS similarity justified? {
=A. No, all three side ratios must be equal.#SSS similarity requires the same ratio for every corresponding side pair.
~B. Yes, two ratios match.#All three ratios must match, not only two.
~C. Yes, any ratio greater than 1 works.#The actual ratio value matters less than equality across all pairs.
~D. No, side ratios are never used.#SSS similarity is based on side ratios.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence is enough for AA similarity? {
~A. One pair of sides is congruent.#One side pair alone does not establish similarity.
=B. Two pairs of corresponding angles are congruent.#Two angle pairs establish triangle similarity because the third angle pair must also match.
~C. One side ratio is known.#One ratio alone is insufficient.
~D. The triangles are drawn with the same orientation.#Orientation is not a similarity criterion.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which similarity statement matches A to R, B to S, and C to T? {
~A. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;RTS#This pairs B with T instead of S.
~B. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;SRT#This pairs A with S instead of R.
=C. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;RST#The vertex order correctly pairs A-R, B-S, and C-T.
~D. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;TSR#This reverses the intended correspondence.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation maps one triangle to a triangle congruent to another. What conclusion is supported? {
~A. The triangles have no relationship.#The transformation sequence gives a relationship.
~B. The original triangle must be larger.#Dilation may enlarge or reduce depending on scale factor.
~C. Only one angle is related.#A dilation preserves all angle measures.
=D. The original triangle is similar to the other triangle.#Dilation supports similarity, and congruence-preserving motion can complete the mapping.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar figures have corresponding sides 5 and 20. What is the scale factor from first to second? {
=A. 4#20 divided by 5 equals 4.
~B. 15#This subtracts instead of comparing by ratio.
~C. 25#This adds the side lengths.
~D. &frac14;#This is the reverse scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A similar triangle has scale factor 3. A side of 11 becomes what length? {
~A. 14#Adding 3 does not use scale factor.
=B. 33#Multiply 11 by 3.
~C. 8#Subtracting 3 does not use scale factor.
~D. 3.67#Dividing by 3 scales in reverse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A map scale is 1 inch to 25 miles. How far is 4 inches? {
~A. 29 miles#Adding 4 and 25 does not use the scale.
~B. 6.25 miles#Dividing 25 by 4 reverses the relationship.
=C. 100 miles#Four inches represents 4 times 25 miles.
~D. 75 miles#That would match 3 inches, not 4.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A 5-ft person casts a 6-ft shadow. A tree casts an 18-ft shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the tree? {
~A. 17 ft#Adding 12 to the height copies a difference rather than a ratio.
~B. 21.6 ft#Multiplying by 4.32 does not use the shadow scale factor.
~C. 10 ft#This does not match the scale factor of 3.
=D. 15 ft#The shadow scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; 5 times 3 is 15.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two congruent figures are similar with what scale factor? {
=A. 1#Conguent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1.
~B. 0#A scale factor of 0 collapses lengths.
~C. 2#A scale factor of 2 changes size.
~D. &frac12;#A scale factor of &frac12; changes size.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model car is 12 cm long at a 1:20 scale. What is the actual length? {
~A. 32 cm#Adding 20 does not use the scale ratio.
=B. 240 cm#Actual length is 20 times the model length: 12 times 20.
~C. 8 cm#Subtracting 20 is not meaningful here.
~D. 0.6 cm#Dividing by 20 scales in the wrong direction.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A drawing uses scale factor &frac15;. An actual length is 60 cm. What is the drawing length? {
~A. 55 cm#Subtracting 5 does not use scale.
~B. 65 cm#Adding 5 does not use scale.
=C. 12 cm#60 times &frac15; equals 12.
~D. 300 cm#This uses the reciprocal scale and scales up.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>If corresponding sides are 7 to 14 and 9 to x, what is x? {
~A. 16#Adding 7 repeats a difference rather than a scale.
~B. 11#Adding 2 does not use the scale factor.
~C. 4.5#Dividing by 2 scales in the wrong direction.
=D. 18#The scale factor is 2, so 9 becomes 18.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 3 and 4. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 5#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 5.
~B. 7#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 1#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 12#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 17#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 13#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 13.
~C. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 60#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 23#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=C. 17#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 17.
~D. 120#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 7 and 24. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 31#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 17#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 168#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=D. 25#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 25.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 9 and 40. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 41#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 41.
~B. 49#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 31#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 360#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 12 and 16. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 28#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 20#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 20.
~C. 4#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 192#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines sine? {
~A. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines cosine.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This defines tangent.
=C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine compares the opposite side to the hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This reverses sine.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines cosine? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines sine.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This defines tangent.
~C. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses cosine.
=D. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines tangent? {
=A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines sine.
~C. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines cosine.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This uses the wrong denominator for tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 13. What is sin A? {
~A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#This would use adjacent over hypotenuse.
=B. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This would define tangent if 12 were adjacent.
~D. <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>#This reverses sine.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle B, adjacent = 8 and hypotenuse = 17. What is cos B? {
~A. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#This would be sine if 15 were opposite.
~B. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This would be tangent with opposite over adjacent.
=C. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>17</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle C, opposite = 7 and adjacent = 24. What is tan C? {
~A. <sup>24</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#This reverses tangent.
~B. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>#This would be sine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>24</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>#This would be cosine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
=D. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp forms a right triangle with legs 6 ft and 8 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
=A. 10 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 10 ft.
~B. 14 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 2 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 48 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A support beam forms a right triangle with legs 9 ft and 12 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 21 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 15 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 15 ft.
~C. 3 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 108 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder brace forms a right triangle with legs 12 ft and 16 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 28 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 4 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=C. 20 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 20 ft.
~D. 192 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal walkway forms a right triangle with legs 15 ft and 20 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 35 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 5 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 300 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
=D. 25 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 25 ft.
}
